2.2.11.m:
The sacrificer prospers; he prospers with offspring;

he prospers with the Brahman, who has a brahman priest possessing such knowledge.

The gods and the asuras contended. Those gods were performing in the sacrifice identically [with the asuras]. Whatever the gods did, that the asuras did. They did not achieve distinction. Those gods said: 'Bring this sacrifice aside, above the asuras; let us extend it.' Covering it with these [formulas], they stepped out: 'The sacrificial formulas in the sacrifice, the fuel sticks, svāhā!' They extended that sacrifice aside, above the asuras. The asuras did not follow this sacrifice of theirs. Thereupon the gods flourished; the asuras were defeated. He who, knowing thus, extends the sacrifice aside, above the asuras, flourishes himself; his disliked rival is defeated. One should offer with these very [formulas] at a colliding sacrifice, reciting them four by four. One should offer [them] before the morning recitation. As great as the sacrifice is, as great as this sacrifice is, that he appropriates. He becomes endowed with the sacrifice; the other becomes without the sacrifice. With these very [formulas] one should offer before the twelve-day sacrifice. This twelve-day sacrifice is indeed visible. Having touched it, one should offer with these very [formulas] before the consecration. This consecration is indeed visible. Having touched it, one should touch the guest offering with these very [formulas], [saying]: 'With the sacrifice the gods sacrificed to the sacrifice.'

2.2.17.m:
Regarding this they say: 'Now why do they offer the choice oblations only in the Soma sacrifice, and not in the oblation sacrifice?'
Incomplete indeed is this worship of the gods, namely the oblation sacrifice;
but complete is this worship of the gods, namely the Soma sacrifice.
Therefore they offer the choice oblations only in the Soma sacrifice.
'May I be pleasing to speech, pleasing to the lord of speech; O divine speech, what is sweetest in speech, in that place me; svāhā to speech, svāhā to the lord of speech, svāhā to Sarasvatī, svāhā to Sarasvatī'—thus he offers with the svāhā-call at the beginning.
Therefore, speech, emitted from then upward, conveys the sacrifice, and the subsequent [part] with the mind;
for mind is pleased by mind.
And on this point some offer seven oblations, saying, 'Seven metres are set in motion with each formula.'
Just as a girdle is cast around for separating the pure and the impure, even so are these [mounds] cast down for separating the pure and the impure, for separating the sacrifice.
For east of the hearths are the worlds of the gods; west of them, those of men.
Therefore, drinking Soma, one should not approach the hearths facing east;
for this is [the realm of mortal] people;
for they ascend to the world of the gods.
This is their abode and their place of emergence, namely the Āgnīdhra and the Sadas.
If one who does not know traverses it, he incurs misfortune;
but if one who knows traverses it, he does not incur misfortune concerning the hearths.

2.2.20.m:
Now, if he should wish of anyone, "May he die," he should alienate him from these;

On this they say: 'Since the sacrifice belongs to Indra, why then at the morning soma-pressing do only two [priests] sacrifice directly with verses to Indra for the set-off offerings, namely the Hotṛ and the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin?'
With 'This sweet soma-drink is for you,' the Hotṛ sacrifices;
With 'Indra, you the bull, we,' the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin [sacrifices];
The others [sacrifice] with [verses] addressed to various deities.
How do their [verses] become addressed to Indra?
With 'We invoke Mitra,' the Maitrāvaruṇa sacrifices,
'And Varuṇa to drink soma.'
Whatever has 'drinking' is a form of Indra;
Thereby he pleases Indra.
With 'In whose house the Maruts,' the Potṛ sacrifices,
'That man is most well guarded.'
Indra indeed is the guardian;
That is a form of Indra;
Thereby he pleases Indra.
With 'O Agni, bring the wives here,' the Neṣṭṛ sacrifices,
'And Tvaṣṭṛ to drink soma.'
Whatever has 'drinking' is a form of Indra;
Thereby he pleases Indra.
With 'To him whose food is oxen, whose food is barren cows,' the Āgnīdhra sacrifices,
'To the wise one whose back is soma.'
Indra indeed is the wise one;
That is a form of Indra;
Thereby he pleases Indra.

'Come with the gods who travel at dawn, possessing noble wealth, O Indra and Agni, to drink soma.'

[This verse] of the Acchāvāka is self-complete.

In this way, indeed, these [verses] become addressed to Indra.

In that they are addressed to various deities, thereby he pleases the other deities.

2.2.21.m:
In that they are in the gāyatrī metre, thereby they belong to Agni.

Therefore, by means of these, a threefold [benefit] is attained.

At the midday pressing, however, all indeed sacrifice with direct verses to Indra for the libations set forward;
some [verses] are piercing.
The Hotṛ sacrifices with: "Drink the soma, pierce [it] as a mighty one...";
the Maitrāvaruṇa with: "Drink it, you who are dreg-loving, victorious...";
the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin with: "Thus drink as of old, let it gladden you...";
the Potṛ with: "Come hither, they call you desirous of soma...";
the Neṣṭṛ with: "Yours is this soma; come hither...";
the Acchāvāka with: "To Indra the somas, known from of old...";
the Āgnīdhra with: "His beaker is filled full, svāhā!"
Thus indeed these become piercing.
Indra indeed did not conquer the morning pressing;
with these he pierced the midday pressing.
Since with these he pierced the midday pressing, therefore these become piercing.

2.2.22.m:
On this they say: 'Since the evening soma-pressing belongs to Indra and the Ṛbhus, why then at the evening soma-pressing does only one [priest] sacrifice directly with a verse to Indra and the Ṛbhus for the set-off offerings?'
With 'Indra, together with the Ṛbhus, rich in prize-food, sprinkled,' only the Hotṛ [sacrifices];
The others [sacrifice] with [verses] addressed to various deities.
How do their [verses] become addressed to Indra and the Ṛbhus?
With 'O Indra and Varuṇa, drinkers of pressed [soma], this pressed [juice],' the Maitrāvaruṇa sacrifices;
'Your chariot, O sacrifice, for the feast of the gods'—he speaks of many;
That is a form of the Ṛbhus.
With 'O Indra and Bṛhaspati, drink the soma,' the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin sacrifices;
'May the drops enter you, of own nature'—he speaks of many;
That is a form of the Ṛbhus.
With 'May the swift-running coursers bring you,' the Potṛ sacrifices;
'Swift-flying, advance with your arms'—he speaks of many;
That is a form of the Ṛbhus.
With 'Truly to us, easy to invoke, come here,' the Neṣṭṛ sacrifices;
'Come here'—he speaks of many;
That is a form of the Ṛbhus.
With 'O Indra and Viṣṇu, drink of this sweet drink,' the Acchāvāka sacrifices;
'To you have the intoxicating foods come'—he speaks of many;
That is a form of the Ṛbhus.
With 'This praise to the worthy Jātavedas,' the Āgnīdhra sacrifices;
'Like a chariot let us construct with thought'—he speaks of many;
That is a form of the Ṛbhus.
In this way, indeed, these [verses] become addressed to Indra and the Ṛbhus.

In that they are addressed to various deities, thereby he pleases the other deities.

2.3.2.m:
The vaṣaṭ-call is indeed a thunderbolt.
Whomever he might hate, meditating on him with his mind, he should utter the vaṣaṭ-call.
He establishes that thunderbolt upon him.
He utters the vaṣaṭ-call as 'ṣaṭ'.
Six indeed are the seasons,
for the attainment of the seasons.
He utters the vaṣaṭ-call as 'vauṣaṭ'.
'Vāu' is yonder [sun], the seasons are six.
He places him into the seasons;
he establishes him in the seasons.
And Vaida used to say regarding this: 'By this he establishes these six:
Heaven is established in the intermediate space;
the intermediate space in the earth;
the earth in the waters;
the waters in truth;
truth in the Brahman;
the Brahman in ascetic heat.'
These deities, being firm standings, establish themselves, [and] following them he establishes himself in all this;
he who knows thus establishes himself with offspring and cattle. || 2

2.3.20.m:
Now begins the midday [soma-pressing] of the one-day sacrifice.
In the beginning, Agni, this was the ṛc and the sāman.
Sā indeed was the name of the ṛc;
Ama was the name of the sāman.
That ṛc addressed the sāman: "Let the two of us unite as a couple for reproduction."
"No," said the sāman;
"greater than this indeed is my majesty."
Becoming two, they two addressed [it];
it did not respond to them two at all.
Becoming three, they addressed [it].
Because, having become three, they addressed it, then it united with three.
Because it united with three, therefore they praise with three [verses];
they sing the udgītha with three [verses];
for the sāman is measured out by three.
Therefore one man has many wives;
one woman does not have many husbands together.
In that Sā and Ama spoke together, that became sāman.
That is the sāman-nature of the sāman.
He becomes harmonious;
he attains supremacy.
He who flourishes indeed becomes harmonious;
"he is without sāman," thus they reproach [him].
They two, becoming five on one side and five on the other, arranged themselves: the invocation and the hiṃkāra, the prastāva and the first ṛc, the udgītha and the middle [ṛc], the pratīhāra and the last [ṛc], the nidhana and the vaṣaṭkāra. Because they two, becoming five on one side and five on the other, arranged themselves, therefore they say: "Fivefold is the sacrifice,"

And since they two amounted to the virāj having ten [parts], therefore they say: "The sacrifice is established in the virāj having ten [parts]."

And when he begins with the bṛhatī metre—he who shines here indeed belongs to the bṛhatī metre—

thereby he prospers him with his own form.

2.3.21.m:
Taking it up again, he makes the two into three,

The stotriya verse is the self;
the anurūpa verse is the offspring;
the wife is the dhāyyā verse;
cattle are the pragātha;
the houses are the hymn;
what is inside the self is the nivit;
firm standing is the concluding verse; food is the yājyā verse.
He exists in this world and in yonder world, and thrives in offspring, in cattle, and in houses, who knows thus.

2.4.4.m:
Now, saying: 'Adhvaryu, let us two recite!'—[they invoke] for the stotriya hymn, for the anurūpa, for the pragātha, for the ukthamukha, and for the concluding verse: thus they invoke five times.
The paṅkti [metre] has five feet;
the sacrifice is fivefold.
All recite verses to Indra in the triṣṭubh metre,
for the midday soma-pressing belongs to Indra and is in the triṣṭubh metre.
All conclude with verses containing the word 'together' (samavatī);
as to why they conclude with verses containing the word 'together', the final round (paryāsa) is the end, the conclusion (udarka) is the end;
with the end, indeed, they conclude the end.
All sacrifice with verses containing the word 'exhilarating' (madvatī);
as to why they sacrifice with verses containing the word 'exhilarating', all sacrifice with verses containing the word 'pressed' (sutavatī), with verses containing the word 'drunk' (pītavatī), which are appropriate;
what is appropriate at the sacrifice, that is successful.
All perform the secondary vaṣaṭ call;
the secondary vaṣaṭ call is of the nature of the sviṣṭakṛt oblation;
[it is done] lest they should interrupt what has been properly performed.
The midday soma-pressing is the world of the intermediate space;
five are its quarters;
five are the uktha hymns of the midday soma-pressing;
by these five uktha hymns he obtains these five quarters, he obtains these five quarters.

2.4.5.m:
Now, when the domestic fires are attended to at the evening soma-pressing, he thereby pleases the ancestors.
The Āgnīdhra priest sacrifices the oblation to [Agni] Pātnīvata in a low voice.
The [oblation to] Pātnīvata is semen;
semen is emitted, as it were, in a low voice.
He does not perform a secondary vaṣaṭ call, lest he should bring to an end the semen that has been emitted;
for the semen that has been emitted is successful when not brought to an end, as it were.
The secondary vaṣaṭ call is indeed an ending;
therefore he does not perform a secondary vaṣaṭ call.
Sitting on the lap of the Neṣṭṛ priest or at the edge of the dhiṣṇya hearth, he consumes [his share].
The Neṣṭṛ priest is indeed the vessel of the wives;
the Āgnīdhra deposits semen into the wives.
From the semen that has been emitted, offspring are born;
[it is] for the procreation of offspring.
He becomes possessed of offspring and capable of procreating;
[it is] for procreation.
He is propagated with offspring and cattle, who knows this.

2.4.6.m:
Now they offer the Śākala oblations.
Just as a snake frees itself from its sloughed-off skin, or a reed stalk from the muñja grass, even so are they entirely freed from all evil, who offer the Śākala oblations.
There are roasted grains in the droṇakalaśa trough;
they take them with their hands.
Roasted grains are cattle;
they pour them out at the edge of the ashes of the Āhavanīya fire.
The Āhavanīya fire is the womb of cattle;
they place them in their own cattle-pen, so as not to depart.
Then, turning to the left, they swell the soma portions in the waters.
They set them down inside the vedi;
for that is the soma's dwelling-place.
West of the cātvāla pit, the Adhvaryu, having filled the cups with water, placing them facing north, inserts fresh grass blades in between.
When waters and herbs come together, then soma becomes complete.
They pour them down with a verse addressed to Viṣṇu;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he establishes him in the sacrifice in the end.
Now, in that they make a substitute for the portion to be consumed, they thereby conceal the divine portion with the human portion to be consumed.

2.4.7.m:
It is putrid indeed that carries the Adhvaryu and the sacrificer to the other world.
If they were to bring it to the avabhṛtha bath without offering upon it with curd, it would make him just as when a corpse emits a smell.
Now, in that they bring it to the avabhṛtha bath after offering upon it with curd, he extends it all, having its womb;
they bring about success.
He offers, reciting: 'God Savitṛ has now become worthy of our praise.'
He collects it all with its joints;
with three [verses],
for the sacrifice is threefold.
He offers over it with verses containing the word 'drop' (drapsavatī);
he collects it all with all its limbs.
He offers over it with verses addressed to Soma;
he collects it all with its self.
He offers over it with five [verses];
the sacrifice is fivefold;
he secures the sacrifice.
Man is fivefold;
he attains man.
Cattle are fivefold;
he is firmly established in cattle.
He is firmly established with offspring and cattle, who knows this.

2.5.11.m:
Now therefore the non-one-day rite (anaikāhika).
They make the stotriya of the morrow correspond to the stotriya of today at the morning pressing;
they thereby continue the Ahīna;
for the continuity of the Ahīna.
Just as the pressing is of a one-day rite, so is the pressing of the Ahīna;
just as the pressings of a pressed one-day rite proceed to their conclusion, so the days of a pressed Ahīna proceed to their conclusion.
In that they make the stotriya of the morrow correspond to the stotriya of today at the morning pressing,
they thereby make one day correspond to another day;
by the following day they take up the next day.
Not so at the midday pressing:
the pṛṣṭhas are prosperity;
they remain fixed in that alone.
By this same rule at the third pressing;
they do not make the stotriya of the morrow correspond to the stotriya of today.
